      },
      "text": "Representation theory studies how groups act on vector spaces — it is the bridge between abstract algebraic symmetry and concrete linear algebra. It is the mathematical language of particle physics (SU(2), SU(3), the Standard Model), quantum mechanics (angular momentum, spin), and crystallography. More recently it is the theoretical foundation for equivariant neural networks and geometric deep learning. From your MIT background in groups and linear algebra, you have all the prerequisites — this guide builds from finite groups to Lie groups to the physics and ML applications."
    },
    {
      "id": "24-REPRESENTATION-THEORY.md#003",
      "path": [
        "24-REPRESENTATION-THEORY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mathematics:24-representation-theory, git-history:mathematics:24-representation-theory]",
        "canonical_path": "mathematics/24-REPRESENTATION-THEORY.md",
        "concepts": "[representation, theory]",
        "current_path": "mathematics/24-REPRESENTATION-THEORY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mathematics:representation-theory",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mathematics",
